WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.520
 [MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:20.840
 I was hired at Netscape in April 1995.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.720
 Netscape had already launched its 1.0 Mosaic killer.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.640
 If you remember, NTSA Mosaic was the big browser

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.000
 until Netscape took over.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.480
 So when I came to Netscape, they'd

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:32.760
 been going for about a year.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.920
 I actually had an option to go in at the beginning,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:35.680
 and I passed it up.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.360
 But I went in in time to do what I

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.320
 was tempted to do, which is a programming language for HTML

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.320
 for web designers and programmers to use embedded directly

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.360
 in the web page, not something that was like coming along

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.360
 at the time called Java, which was more of a professional's

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.960
 language where you would write real code with type

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:54.680
 declarations.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.320
 And you'd have to write that code in a way that compiled.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.280
 I was writing something JavaScript

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.040
 that could be used by people who didn't know what a compiler was.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:03.960
 They were just going to load it.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:04.920
 It was like Basic.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.200
 And that was really the pitch, that we would make two languages,

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.160
 not one, analogous to Microsoft's Visual Basic for C++.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.800
 So JavaScript for Java.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.680
 Bill Joy of Sun actually liked that idea and agreed with it.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.600
 And he was the guy that signed the trademark license by which

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.320
 what I had created became JavaScript.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.760
 The name is a total lie.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.400
 It's not really related to Java so much

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.840
 as to a common ancestor C in syntax.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.320
 And to the extent that we made it easy to use and something

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.680
 you could copy paste program or start small from scripts

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.360
 and grow to programs, JavaScript has succeeded massively.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.920
 It was also an incredible rush job.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:44.760
 So there were mistakes in it.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:49.280
 And something that I think is important about it

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.600
 is that I knew there would be mistakes

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:52.640
 and there would be gaps.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.200
 So I made it very malleable as a language.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.120
 And that has enabled web developers

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.320
 to make it be what they want it to be,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.560
 to project their own style of not just API,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.280
 but almost language pattern on it,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.240
 and create their own innovation networks,

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.840
 to use Eric Von Hippel's phrase, or innovation toolkits on top

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:11.400
 of it.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.440
 So it's not a language that tries to restrict you

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:14.240
 to one paradigm.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.640
 It's a multi-paradigm language.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.440
 I mean, there's all these things where a language comes out

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.400
 and immediately trips and falls.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.400
 And you have to sort of-- it's OK the second time.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.760
 But there wasn't really a trip and fall in JavaScript.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:26.640
 Why?

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.880
 So you could say that it hasn't been a version, too.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:29.840
 And that's true, because I've tried

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:31.960
 to work on what might be a big version, too.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:33.560
 That was called the fourth edition.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.280
 And that failed.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.400
 There has been evolution.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.680
 The web is all about evolution.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.200
 People don't quite see this while you're in the midst of it,

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.760
 because it's microevolution.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.880
 But web pages from the '90s don't all

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.080
 render properly these days.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.120
 They don't all work right.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.400
 And a lot of them are lost and only available

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.960
 through webarchive.org.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.280
 JavaScript had enough at the beginning, enough good parts,

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.560
 to use Crockford's phrase, or enough genetic material

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.640
 from other languages, first-class functions,

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.520
 prototypal inheritance from self.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.160
 The inheritance of first-class functions from scheme

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.560
 is really kind of a fraud.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.160
 Because scheme is different in many ways.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.120
 And I couldn't make those differences manifest.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.160
 I couldn't do the scheme thing in JavaScript.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.680
 I was under these marching orders

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.480
 to make it look like Java.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.280
 I had 10 days to prototype it.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:25.180
 So scheme was more of a spiritual

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.440
 than an actual influence.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.280
 But first-class functions are very powerful.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.360
 And they fit with an event handling sort of programming

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.160
 model.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.600
 I was inspired by Atkinson's hypercard.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.680
 So that's why you see onclick in JavaScript.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.600
 Hypercard had this pattern for event handlers

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.600
 called on, on page, down, or whatever.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:49.280
 So JavaScript had enough good at the beginning to survive.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.920
 Now, if you think back, though, to the mid '90s,

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:52.960
 JavaScript was cursed.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.760
 Because it was mainly used for annoyances,

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.840
 like little scrolling messages in the status bar

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.320
 at the bottom of your browser, or flashing images,

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:01.740
 or things that popped up windows massively.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.400
 We could have put in controls for those.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:04.120
 And we should have.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.620
 Eventually, browsers, Firefox kind of championed this,

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.800
 led this automatic suppression of annoyances

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.520
 that made it all much better.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.480
 And with Moore's law compounding, and with JavaScript

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.520
 getting some evolutionary improvements in the standards

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:20.080
 process, it became really quite fast enough and good enough

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.960
 in 2004 and '05 to beget the Web 2.0 revolution.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:28.240
 That was, I think, tied in with Firefox's retaking market

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.400
 share from IE, and developers realizing

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.080
 there was a client side to the programming stack that

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.600
 could be expressive and powerful,

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.600
 and could be fast enough thanks to faster computers, mainly.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.040
 You must have had some training, some kind of--

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.080
 Oh, I've done a lot of--

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:43.960
 --experiences that kind of got to the point

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:47.420
 where you could pull from Scheme and--

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.760
 I had implemented-- I was sort of a language buff

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.260
 when I first entered computer science.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.520
 I was a math physics major, originally,

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:55.960
 and ended up in math computer science

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.920
 when I finally got my undergraduate degree.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:03.160
 So I was programming formal language theory applied

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:08.560
 to recognizing languages, lexical analysis, parsers,

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:10.820
 automatically constructed parsers from grammars.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:14.000
 I love that stuff, because it was all very pretty and clean,

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:14.640
 theoretically.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:15.840
 And it still is.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.560
 It hasn't changed a lot.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:20.360
 There's been only one or two innovations since my time

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.000
 in university in the early '80s.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.440
 So what that gave me was the ability

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.280
 to quickly knock out a sort of a language interpreter.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.000
 I could do the parser and the scanner.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.160
 I could generate bytecode, because Netscape wanted

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.240
 to do a server-side embedding in JavaScript,

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.680
 even though it could have been TreeWalker, something

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.480
 that interpreted parser, and I made a bytecode for it.

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:46.080
 And it was an internal bytecode, not the Java bytecode that's

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.120
 become a handicap for Java, I think.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.080
 And I knocked all that out really quickly,

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:52.200
 because I'd done it before.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.280
 I'd done it at Silicon Graphics to build sort of network

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:57.200
 monitoring tools to capture packets based on expressions

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.560
 over fields at the various protocol headers.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.480
 I'd done it for fun, just to make my own languages.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.680
 And finally, I got to do it really quickly.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.000
 The speed was an issue for me.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.800
 It was partly-- we were all feeling like Microsoft

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.320
 was going to come after Netscape,

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.600
 because they had tried to buy Netscape in late '94

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:15.480
 for too little money.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:18.040
 I'd heard about this before my time at Netscape.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:21.720
 But we also were in a weird game theory with respect to Java,

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:23.840
 because even at Netscape, some people thought, well,

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.520
 if we have Java, do we really need a second language?

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:30.240
 They didn't see the benefit of the visual basic companion

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.000
 language for a much larger cohort of programmers

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.440
 or amateurs, designers, beginners.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.480
 To write Java as to write C++ for the Microsoft platform

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.960
 today took a lot of education and greater pay.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.880
 It was a higher price proposition.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:48.360
 To get people gluing components together and designing pages

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:49.840
 and filling gaps using JavaScript,

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.520
 as they did with Visual Basic and Microsoft's Windows,

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:56.440
 was cheaper and wider spread.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:01.000
 It also enabled this user innovation toolkit approach,

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.120
 to use Von Hebel's phrase again, because JavaScript

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.280
 was malleable, because there were so many web designers,

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.200
 you would see different schools of thought

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.480
 on how to use it emerge.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.680
 And this has become quite clear over the last 10 years

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:13.320
 with the various JavaScript libraries.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:14.560
 And I think that's actually an advantage,

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.160
 as I said earlier, to JavaScript,

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.600
 that we're not telling you, here's the one way to write it.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.880
 Here's the one true object-oriented paradigm.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.200
 Here's the only way you should ever make a reusable abstraction.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:25.280
 It's not unmixed, right?

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:26.320
 It's hard for beginners.

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.680
 People reinvent certain wheels and make mistakes doing it

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.040
 or don't like having to acquire a library.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.000
 But you see jQuery, a very popular library,

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.840
 because it gives people this very sweet query-and-do paradigm.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:40.600
 And again, it's not mandatory with JavaScript,

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.520
 but a lot of people learn that, and they think that is JavaScript.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:43.920
 They think jQuery is a language,

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:47.160
 or they think jQuery is the tail that wagged a dog.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:50.440
 jQuery is great, and John Reznik used to work with us at Mozilla.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.320
 But there are so many good libraries out there now,

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.920
 and they're actually shrinking and becoming more compositional,

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:56.240
 which is a good trend.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:00.640
 So JavaScript, by being malleable and fostering user innovation,

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.200
 I think has played a unique role.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:04.600
 If I had done something more rigid,

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:08.120
 I think the odds are greater it would have failed.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:10.280
 -I just can't imagine how you would have escaped

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:14.440
 from the object-oriented pattern of C++.

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:18.280
 -Partly I had to, because if I had done classes in JavaScript

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:21.000
 back in those 10 days in May in 1995,

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.920
 I think I would have been told this is too much like Java.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.480
 You're competing with Java.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.960
 Somebody at Sun would have yelled at Bill Joy more than they did,

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:29.120
 and it might have killed the deal.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:32.320
 So I was definitely not only under time constraint

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.480
 but under marketing orders, make it look like Java,

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:36.440
 but don't make it too big for its bridges.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:40.520
 It's just this sort of silly little brother language, right?

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:41.920
 The sidekick to Java.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:43.880
 -But then you put in primitives.

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:45.120
 -I stuck some stuff in, yeah.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.200
 -You put some primitives, like closures and all those other things,

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.320
 that it's like you can build what you want.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:54.480
 -Yes, and that kind of went under the radar for a lot of people.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.880
 It wasn't all even there in a good working order in the first release.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:02.480
 But over the next few years, it became not only well-known,

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:04.640
 I would say more standardized than well-known.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.520
 Over the next 10 years, it became evangelized,

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:08.920
 like Crockford's a big exponent of the closure pattern

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:11.160
 and the good uses you can make of closures.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.760
 So people find the malleability and the expressiveness and the power

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:18.080
 compelling enough that some people actually resist any version 2.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:23.640
 They say, "I don't want you to add cliches or common special forms

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:30.480
 for patterns that I'm more happy writing myself or acquiring as a library myself."

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:34.200
 -You've created an abstraction that implementers can do crazy things, right?

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:37.840
 And people can rethink what the interpreter is really supposed to do.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:38.320
 -They can.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.200
 -And they can just say, "Okay, here comes V8,"

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.360
 and a lot of things differently, right?

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.160
 -Optimizations that haven't been tried.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:49.000
 I knew about these optimizations because I'd studied Smalltalk itself,

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:51.240
 but nobody had gotten the time and the money.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.160
 Google was maybe first.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:55.000
 There were other efforts going on in parallel,

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.320
 and Apple and Mozilla have kept up as best they can,

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.440
 but V8 deserves a lot of credit for pushing this forward.

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:04.640
 It wasn't quite as first on the scene as they like to claim

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:07.560
 because it was all coming together in 2008, but it has been very helpful.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.080
 It has shown people what can be done.

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:10.600
 What's interesting to me is that you then go

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:14.360
 and put different, more intensive workloads on the language,

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:17.640
 and you see there's a new V8 that should come out of somewhere.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.480
 It may not come out of Google because they may have tired of optimizing JavaScript.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:25.160
 In fact, I believe Dart is a response to that by the principals who did V8.

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:27.200
 They want to do a language where they don't have to worry

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:29.920
 about all this crazy compatibility.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.840
 It may not succeed, and it also doesn't give JavaScript

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:36.440
 the next level of performance, but I believe that level is there,

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:39.800
 and it's still improving in performance much more dramatically

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:42.960
 than a language like Java where the gains are a percent

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:45.520
 or a fraction of a percent on the standard benchmarks.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:51.120
 But a lot of HTML5 development now, "HTML5," JavaScript, CSS, web APIs,

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.440
 beyond what's in HTML5, is taking off.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:56.880
 You're seeing like Zynga doing HTML5-only games.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:59.120
 It's really coming faster than some thought,

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:02.480
 and I talked to a venture capitalist, Fred Wilson of Union Square in New York,

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.120
 said, "Yeah, it's here." He thought it would take years.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:07.360
 We've turned that corner, and so you call it HTML5.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.240
 What it really means is it's the web stack.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:12.640
 It's the same stack you use to write web pages and host web apps.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:17.000
 You can write apps that run in your device, apps that are maybe hosted,

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:20.640
 maybe offline, maybe the line is blurred so that you can associate them

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:23.320
 with a URL, but you can also take them on the plane without any fear

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:26.600
 that you're going to lose anything by disconnecting from the Internet.

